<h2>
                        What You Will Learn
                    </h2>
<ol>
<li>Define memory management and its purpose.</li>
<li>Identify different types of computer memory.</li>
<li>Explain operating system memory management.</li>
<li>Understand allocation and deallocation.</li>
<li>Differentiate physical and virtual memory.</li>
<li>Explain paging, segmentation, and swapping.</li>
<li>Understand page tables and address translation.</li>
<li>Recognize page faults and replacement algorithms.</li>
<li>Explain the role of virtual memory in multitasking.</li>
<li>Identify memory management problems and solutions.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     PART 1 — MEMORY MANAGEMENT
                     SECTION 1.1
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-1">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 1 • SECTION 1.1
                    </span>
<h2>
                        What Is Memory Management?
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Memory management is the process of controlling and
                        organizing a computer's memory. It is one of the
                        main responsibilities of an operating system.
                    </p>
<p>
                        When a program runs, it needs memory to store instructions,
                        data, and temporary information. The operating system
                        determines how much memory the program can use.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Memory management also helps prevent programs from
                        accessing or changing another program's information
                        without permission.
                    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>

                        Google Chrome, Microsoft Word, and a music player
                        may all require memory at the same time.
                        The operating system distributes memory resources
                        so they can run together.

                    </div>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 1.2
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-2">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 1 • SECTION 1.2
                    </span>
<h2>
                        Importance of Memory Management
                    </h2>
<h3>1. Efficient Memory Usage</h3>
<p>
                        Helps the operating system use available memory
                        properly and avoid unnecessary waste.
                    </p>
<h3>2. Supports Multitasking</h3>
<p>
                        Allows several applications to run at the same
                        time by distributing memory resources.
                    </p>
<h3>3. Protects Programs</h3>
<p>
                        Helps prevent unauthorized access to memory
                        belonging to other processes.
                    </p>
<h3>4. Improves System Performance</h3>
<p>
                        Organizes memory so applications can access
                        the information they require.
                    </p>
<h3>5. Prevents Memory Conflicts</h3>
<p>
                        Reduces the risk of programs incorrectly
                        using the same memory locations.
                    </p>
<h3>6. Supports Program Execution</h3>
<p>
                        Provides memory for loading and running applications.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 1.3
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-3">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 1 • SECTION 1.3
                    </span>
<h2>
                        How Memory Management Works
                    </h2>
<ol>
<li>A user opens an application.</li>
<li>The operating system receives the request.</li>
<li>The system checks available memory.</li>
<li>Required memory is allocated.</li>
<li>The application uses its assigned memory.</li>
<li>Memory can be released when no longer needed.</li>
</ol>
<p>
                        For example, running a Java program in an IDE requires
                        memory for the development environment, Java runtime,
                        and application itself.
                    </p>
</section>
