<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 1.6
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-6">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 1 • SECTION 1.6
                    </span>
<h2>
                        Types of Memory in a Computer
                    </h2>
<h3>A. Primary Memory</h3>
<p>
                        Primary memory is directly accessible by the CPU
                        and supports current computer operations.
                    </p>
<h3>Random Access Memory (RAM)</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Temporarily stores active data and instructions.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Supports fast reading and writing.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Usually loses data when power is removed.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Helps applications operate efficiently.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Read-Only Memory (ROM)</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Retains information without electrical power.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Commonly associated with firmware.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Supports startup and hardware initialization.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>B. Secondary Memory</h3>
<p>
                        Secondary memory provides long-term storage.
                        Common examples include:
                    </p>
<ul>
<li>Hard Disk Drive (HDD)</li>
<li>Solid-State Drive (SSD)</li>
<li>USB flash drive</li>
<li>Memory card</li>
</ul>
<h3>C. Cache Memory</h3>
<p>
                        Cache is small, high-speed memory located
                        inside or close to the CPU.
                    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Cache</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>L1</td>
<td>Very fast, usually smallest.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>L2</td>
<td>Larger than L1, generally slower.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>L3</td>
<td>Often larger and shared among cores.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>D. CPU Registers</h3>
<p>
                        Registers are tiny storage locations inside the CPU
                        that hold values, addresses, and instructions
                        needed during processing.
                    </p>
<ul>
<li>Program Counter (PC)</li>
<li>Instruction Register (IR)</li>
<li>General-purpose registers</li>
<li>Memory Address Register (MAR)</li>
</ul>
</section>
