<p>
                        Memory closer to the processor is generally
                        faster but more expensive per unit of capacity.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 1.8
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-8">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 1 • SECTION 1.8
                    </span>
<h2>
                        Physical Memory
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Physical memory refers to actual installed memory
                        hardware, particularly RAM.
                    </p>
<p>
                        If a laptop contains 8 GB of RAM, it has
                        8 GB of installed physical RAM.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 1.9
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-9">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 1 • SECTION 1.9
                    </span>
<h2>
                        Logical Memory
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Logical memory refers to the memory address space
                        used by a program during execution.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Programs generally use virtual addresses rather
                        than directly accessing physical RAM locations.
                    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>

                        A program may use virtual address 0x1000.
                        Memory management hardware translates that
                        address into the corresponding physical location.

                    </div>
</section>
