<!-- ==========================================
                     PART 2 — MEMORY ALLOCATION
                     SECTION 2.1
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section2-1">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 2 • SECTION 2.1
                    </span>
<h2>What Is Memory Allocation?</h2>
<p>
                        Memory allocation is the process of assigning
                        available memory space to a program or process.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Allocation can happen when a program starts
                        or during its execution.
                    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
                        A Java application creates an array for 100 integers.
                        Memory must be allocated to hold the array.
                    </div>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 2.2
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section2-2">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 2 • SECTION 2.2
                    </span>
<h2>Types of Memory Allocation</h2>
<h3>A. Static Memory Allocation</h3>
<p>
                        Static allocation involves memory requirements
                        determined in advance for the relevant allocation.
                    </p>
<pre><code>public class StaticExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age = 18;
        double grade = 95.5;

        System.out.println(age);
        System.out.println(grade);
    }
}</code></pre>
<h3>B. Dynamic Memory Allocation</h3>
<p>
                        Dynamic allocation occurs while a program runs,
                        allowing memory to be requested when needed.
                    </p>
<pre><code>public class DynamicExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = new int[5];

        numbers[0] = 10;
        numbers[1] = 20;
        numbers[2] = 30;

        System.out.println(numbers[0]);
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>
                        In Java, the <code>new</code> keyword creates
                        an array object during program execution.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 2.3
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section2-3">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 2 • SECTION 2.3
                    </span>
<h2>What Is Memory Deallocation?</h2>
<p>
                        Memory deallocation makes previously allocated
                        memory available for reuse.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Java uses garbage collection to reclaim memory
                        occupied by objects that are no longer reachable.
                    </p>
<pre><code>public class DeallocationExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] data = new int[100];

        data = null;

        System.out.println("Reference removed.");
    }
}</code></pre>
<div class="note">
                        Assigning <code>null</code> removes the reference.
                        It does not immediately free the memory.
                        The garbage collector may reclaim it later.
                    </div>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 2.4
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section2-4">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 2 • SECTION 2.4
                    </span>
<h2>Why Allocation and Deallocation Matter</h2>
<ul>
<li>Improve memory efficiency.</li>
<li>Provide space for new programs.</li>
<li>Reduce unnecessary memory consumption.</li>
<li>Support multiple running applications.</li>
<li>Maintain system stability.</li>
</ul>
</section>
