                    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     PART 3 — MEMORY MANAGEMENT TECHNIQUES
                     SECTION 3.1
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-1">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.1
                    </span>
<h2>Memory Management Techniques</h2>
<p>
                        Operating systems use different techniques
                        to distribute memory efficiently and
                        reduce memory conflicts.
                    </p>
<ol>
<li>Contiguous memory allocation</li>
<li>Paging</li>
<li>Segmentation</li>
<li>Swapping</li>
<li>Virtual memory</li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.2
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-2">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.2
                    </span>
<h2>Contiguous Memory Allocation</h2>
<p>
                        Contiguous allocation assigns a process
                        one continuous block of physical memory.
                    </p>
<p>
                        If a process requires 100 MB, the system
                        searches for a sufficiently large
                        continuous free block.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Simple to understand.</li>
<li>Easy address calculation.</li>
<li>Efficient access within the allocated block.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>May cause external fragmentation.</li>
<li>Large free blocks may become difficult to find.</li>
<li>Separated free spaces may be wasted.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.3
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-3">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.3
                    </span>
<h2>Types of Contiguous Memory Allocation</h2>
<h3>A. Fixed Partitioning</h3>
<p>
                        Divides memory into predefined sections.
                        Each partition can hold one process at a time.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Simple management.</li>
<li>Easy organization.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Can waste memory inside partitions.</li>
<li>Limits simultaneously allocated processes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>B. Variable Partitioning</h3>
<p>
                        Creates partitions according to the
                        memory requirements of individual processes.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>More flexible allocation.</li>
<li>Can reduce internal fragmentation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>May create small separated free blocks.</li>
<li>May require compaction.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.4
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-4">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.4
                    </span>
<h2>Memory Allocation Strategies</h2>
<h3>First Fit</h3>
<p>
                        Selects the first available memory block
                        large enough for the process.
                    </p>
<h3>Best Fit</h3>
<p>
                        Selects the smallest available block
                        that can accommodate the process.
                    </p>
<h3>Worst Fit</h3>
<p>
                        Selects the largest available block.
                    </p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
                        Available blocks: 100 MB, 300 MB, 500 MB.
                        Required memory: 250 MB.
                    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr>
<th>Strategy</th>
<th>Selected Block</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>First Fit</td>
<td>300 MB</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Best Fit</td>
<td>300 MB</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Worst Fit</td>
<td>500 MB</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
                        First Fit depends on the order
                        in which free blocks are examined.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.5 — PAGING
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-5">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.5
                    </span>
<h2>Paging</h2>
<p>
                        Paging divides virtual memory into
                        fixed-size blocks called pages.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Physical memory is divided into
                        corresponding blocks called frames.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Pages do not need to occupy consecutive
                        physical memory locations.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The operating system uses a page table
                        to track the frames associated with pages.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reduces external fragmentation.</li>
<li>Supports non-contiguous allocation.</li>
<li>Supports virtual memory.</li>
<li>Provides flexible memory allocation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Requires page tables.</li>
<li>May cause internal fragmentation.</li>
<li>Requires address translation.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.6 — PAGES AND FRAMES
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-6">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.6
                    </span>
<h2>Pages and Frames</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Pages</th>
<th>Frames</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Virtual memory blocks</td>
<td>Physical memory blocks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Belong to a process address space</td>
<td>Exist in physical RAM</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fixed size</td>
<td>Same corresponding size</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mapped to frames</td>
<td>Hold resident pages</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
                        With a page size of 4 KB,
                        a process requiring 16 KB needs four pages.
                    </div>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.7 — SEGMENTATION
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-7">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.7
                    </span>
<h2>Segmentation</h2>
<p>
                        Segmentation divides a program into logical
                        sections called segments.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Unlike paging, segments can have
                        different sizes.
                    </p>
<h3>Common Segments</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Code:</strong>
                            Program instructions.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Data:</strong>
                            Global and static information.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Stack:</strong>
                            Function calls and local variables.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Heap:</strong>
                            Dynamically allocated objects.
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Organizes logical program sections.</li>
<li>Supports protection and sharing.</li>
<li>Reflects program structure.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Can cause external fragmentation.</li>
<li>Requires segment tables.</li>
<li>Variable sizes complicate management.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.8 — PAGING VS. SEGMENTATION
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-8">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.8
                    </span>
<h2>Paging vs. Segmentation</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Paging</th>
<th>Segmentation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Fixed-size blocks</td>
<td>Logical variable-size sections</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pages and frames</td>
<td>Segments</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory allocation focus</td>
<td>Program organization focus</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>May cause internal fragmentation</td>
<td>May cause external fragmentation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Uses page tables</td>
<td>Uses segment tables</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.9 — SWAPPING
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-9">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.9
                    </span>
<h2>Swapping</h2>
<p>
                        Swapping temporarily moves memory contents
                        between RAM and secondary storage.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It helps make physical memory available
                        for other processes.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Helps manage limited RAM.</li>
<li>Supports more processes.</li>
<li>Frees physical memory for other activities.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Storage is slower than RAM.</li>
<li>Moving data takes additional time.</li>
<li>Excessive swapping reduces performance.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.10 — INTERNAL FRAGMENTATION
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-10">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.10
                    </span>
<h2>Internal Fragmentation</h2>
<p>
                        Internal fragmentation occurs when
                        an allocated memory block is larger
                        than the memory a process actually needs.
                    </p>
<div class="note">
                        A process requires 18 KB but receives 20 KB.
                        <strong>
                            Unused space = 20 KB − 18 KB = 2 KB.
                        </strong>
</div>
</section>
<!-- ==========================================
                     SECTION 3.11 — EXTERNAL FRAGMENTATION
                ========================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-11">
<span class="chapter-number">
                        PART 3 • SECTION 3.11
                    </span>
<h2>External Fragmentation</h2>
<p>
                        External fragmentation occurs when
                        available memory is divided into
                        separated free blocks.
                    </p>
<p>
